nvoices.js`, `customers.js`, `search.js`) already routed every payload through `toWire`, so the naming inconsistency was not in the handlers — it was in the single boundary helper they all share. `toCamel` in `src/wire.js` only inserted camel humps at separators without normalizing case, so `SCREAMING_SNAKE` keys were mangled:

- `LINE_TOTAL` → `LINETOTAL` (should be `lineTotal`)

Because the mangled key contains no underscore, it even slipped past snake-case detectors while still being wrong for clients — exactly the kind of drift the wire-convention comment warns about. Since `toWire` is the one chokepoint for cart, invoice, customer and search payloads (including nested `lines`, `defaultAddress`, and `hits`), fixing it brings every module onto the convention at once rather than patching each endpoint by hand.

## Fix

In `src/wire.js`, rewrote `toCamel` to split camelCase boundaries first, then split on `_`/`-`/whitespace, lowercase each word, and capitalize every word after the first:

```js
export function toCamel(key) {
  return (
    String(key)
      .replace(/([a-z0-9])([A-Z])/g, '$1_$2')
      .split(/[-_\s]+/)
      .filter(Boolean)
      .map((word, index) => {
        const lower = word.toLowerCase()
        return index === 0 ? lower : lower.charAt(0).toUpperCase() + lower.slice(1)
      })
      .join('')
  )
}
```

Verified behavior: `line_total`, `LINE_TOTAL`, `line-total` → `lineTotal`; `SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE` → `screamingSnakeCase`; already-camelCase keys pass through unchanged (idempotent), and `toWire`/`fromWire` still round-trip storage naming.

